{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-74973" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-74973" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-74973" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha