],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-34456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-34456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ef3ca521064f6da0644188ae9fddb10e4eff435763fcd6e23d30abb70831401"} {"question" "q-34456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-