on is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{aligned}] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second row of the RREF matrix above, we see that the system of equations has no solution.\n\nTherefore, ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-110909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ef3ca521064f6da0644188ae9fddb10e4eff435763fcd6e23d30abb70831401"} {"question" "q-110909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-110909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["text" "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-110909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-75588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-75588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-8,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=7,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-8)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+7\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-8+0+7=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-75588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ef3ca521064f6da0644188ae9fddb10e4eff435763fcd6e23d30abb70831401"} {"question" "q-75588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-75588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "4"] ["text" "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "q-75588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/state.json" "file_sha256" "34151a5639236c16af4187361245fb083bba0831f127c035526d86ff41eb8875"} {"question" "e-4816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vectors ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/308390111ffcb243fb8612b873317302c966df288001b28c6d9b02f5946aac94.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/a7df5d5b19c15a0e5f7b9d1dc93d810ca41b70c84d28c770a0d15521b14d1374.png) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/example-4816.json" "file_sha256" "7ceb08f70f6275ba30095471470c52ebc99ee6e1d94aa72b268d4b1c967a4cdf"} {"question" "e-4816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/e13b964d4391f86125a17302c70af51040c331eb76393ebb39a1fef65dab569d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/example-4816.json" "file_sha256" "7ceb08f70f6275ba30095471470c52ebc99ee6e1d94aa72b268d4b1c967a4cdf"} {"question" "e-6237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vectors ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/ea1fe153d54f6a769e3d13177bfede1eb467525feb255fbcfd7ec88efe58b733.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/075599a7512d1105b9c6ad3acf3f4d043cb60c2aedeb8f53532045390c29f1e9.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/73b2b16a58acd290d040047339b0db3c79de19c0af04303e010a2d16e75bb94b.png) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/example-6237.json" "file_sha256" "0e1f70ce569c83538320e8bd7385641b897d2dd198a6022210f812ac730c4abc"} {"question" "e-6237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/c5383ad51988564756ba754cbd6107f487fd0b32be0013f8a89bb78a6900f540.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/example-6237.json" "file_sha256" "0e1f70ce569c83538320e8bd7385641b897d2dd198a6022210f812ac730c4abc"} {"question" "e-6257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3,$ where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/1d282bb39042b44ed346503f3c7c4213b02089bbc7d833e82a5d95eb49f4db28.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/example-6257.json" "file_sha256" "0d3d005c9e8030ded7cc32b1648d510f176e2a99c5a5308a954a10495e661e86"} {"question" "e-6257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/a23531cb53a9b53f47d8a7080af7de78e6d7cdd7fa3a3632c37a720c7e8ba92c.png)\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5,$ $x_2=0,$ and $x_3=7,$ meaning that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/ca123e2e8451fab6c7b106a5d1a05d153020854c694ec41cfb5d38de2fb3ae5f.png)\n\nTherefore, $x_1 + x_2 +  x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/example-6257.json" "file_sha256" "0d3d005c9e8030ded7cc32b1648d510f176e2a99c5a5308a954a10495e661e86"} {"question" "q-35694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "f5d64874bfc69e482b4f0bffc88ad93a2f3852d24dba5085951cc52f3ee2505f" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment from the original vectors and authentic solution: a2=-2a1, so removing a2 preserves the span. The determinant of the matrix with columns a1 and a3 is (-2)(-3)-(-2)(3)=12, proving independence. Thus {a1,a3}, original option 1, is a basis. Saved grading reports Correct. The stored bmatrix expression contains exactly the same two vectors." "bindings" {"problem" "eb37c33f24b3c5ec73dd791c4c4e0d1048627b507faec60fbab535b2629b4b8f" "worked_solution" "d5567cb48087d8d97a945b9ddaff51a4b1bd36555b9c28052b6eab4521790fbb" "original_field" "2f1e500ba3b3d710da916cc0264a960d7e4e31a976eda144aab1f8b38704df3b" "reviewed_field" "79a0bc26078af887fff156223d52e49379110d3fcd1b37b9b534f3b1d6fdb603" "raw_widget" "852f9defb1b20fb09430c0c93c160533635c35875e951ea009e2bc64b698d549"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-35694-before.png" "sha256" "dfc1e2a9de38ea79bfc2b8e58926d10b18dda19e7cf08d10d97c59223387e0e1"} {"path" "history-q-35694.png" "sha256" "478aabad13fb0edc3e6d07f5019f68ad8a9dfe40950c69f5934d8b03694b3b02"} {"path" "q-35694-after.json" "sha256" "1b644d8e1afde3d3898a278036e194cebc7daa9460207c63a8634a4cde12c9a8"}]} {"question" "q-35694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "f5d64874bfc69e482b4f0bffc88ad93a2f3852d24dba5085951cc52f3ee2505f" "rationale" "Derived judgment from the original vectors and authentic solution: a2=-2a1, so removing a2 preserves the span. The determinant of the matrix with columns a1 and a3 is (-2)(-3)-(-2)(3)=12, proving independence. Thus {a1,a3}, original option 1, is a basis. Saved grading reports Correct. The stored bmatrix expression contains exactly the same two vectors."}] "usage" {"q-35694" [] "q-75588" [] "e-4816" [] "e-6237" [] "e-6257" []} "basis" 2121 "retractions" [[17592186120326 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, $a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$, and $a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a basis $B$ of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$."] [17592186120326 :question/answer-fields 17592186118431] [17592186120451 :question/problem "The vectors $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, $a_{3}$, and $a_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is a basis of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, a_{4}}$. If that is the case, find $x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}$, where $a_{4} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3}$.\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, a_{4} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120451 :question/answer-fields 17592186119181] [17592186119553 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection."] [17592186119553 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S$.\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So\n\n$$\nS = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}\n$$\n\nis spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$.\n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S$.\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119596 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$."] [17592186119596 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{5}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$. So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119597 :question/problem "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$. If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119597 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$. So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + x_{3} = 2; - x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} =-2; - 3x_{1} - 2x_{3} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$.\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5$, $x_2=0$, and $x_3=7$, meaning that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 + x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118432 17592186118433 17592186118434 17592186118435 17592186118436 17592186119182 17592186119183 17592186119184 17592186119185 17592186119186] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118431 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118431 :answer-field/id] [17592186118431 :answer-field/key] [17592186118431 :answer-field/type] [17592186119181 :answer-field/correct] [17592186119181 :answer-field/id] [17592186119181 :answer-field/key] [17592186119181 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
